<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Todo APP</title>
    {{!-- Assume that we are inside static folder when we include this --}}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/style.css">
</head>

<body>

    <div class="container">
        <h1>Todo List</h1>

        <form action="/todos" method="POST">
            <input type="text" name="task" id="name" placeholder="Enter Task Name">
            <input type="text" name="description" id="description" placeholder="Enter description">
            <button type="submit">ADD TODO</button>
        </form>

        <ul class="taskList">
            {{#each todos as |t|}}
            <li class="task-item">
                <a href="/todos/{{t.id}}" target="_blank">
                    <span class="task-name">
                        {{t.task}} <br>
                        {{t.description}}
                    </span>
                </a>

                <a href="/delete-todo/{{t.id}}">
                    <button>❌</button>
                </a>
            </li>
            {{/each}}
        </ul>
    </div>

</body>

</html>

{{!-- 
HBS = Handlebars
A template engine used mainly with Express.js to render HTML on the server.

What it does (simple explanation)
Lets you write HTML pages that contain variables and logic (like loops, conditions).
Express fills in the data and sends the final HTML to the browser.
Similar to EJS or Pug templates.

🔧 HBS Example
template.hbs
<h1>Hello {{name}}</h1>

<ul>
  {{#each items}}
    <li>{{this}}</li>
  {{/each}}
</ul>

Express code:
res.render('template', {
  name: "Bob",
  items: ["apple", "banana", "mango"]
});

Output:
<h1>Hello Bob</h1>
<ul>
  <li>apple</li>
  <li>banana</li>
  <li>mango</li>
</ul>
___________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________

EJS stands for Embedded JavaScript.
It’s a template engine for Node/Express that lets you write HTML pages with JavaScript inside them.
🚀 Simple Explanation
Think of EJS as HTML + JavaScript mixed together so the server can fill in data before sending it to the browser.

ex -> 
🧩 Tiny Example :--

index.ejs:
<h1>Hello <%= name %></h1>

Express code:
res.render('index', { name: "Alice" });

Browser sees:
Hello Alice
___________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________

| Feature        | **EJS**                                        | **HBS (Handlebars)**                        |
| -------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Syntax Style   | Looks like **JavaScript inside HTML**          | Looks like **mustache templates** (`{{ }}`) |
| Logic          | Allows **full JS code** (loops, if, functions) | **Very limited logic** (helpers needed)     |
| Learning Curve | Easy if you know JS                            | Easy even for non-coders                    |
| Flexibility    | More powerful                                  | More strict & safer                         |
| Template Feel  | Feels like coding                              | Feels like pure templating                  |
| Common Use     | Developer-heavy projects                       | Clean & simple templates (blogs, emails)    |
 --}}